+# Hegel property-based testing report: `edn-rs`
+
+## 1. Summary
+
+Added 10 property-based tests plus 5 deterministically-failing known-bug pin
+tests and 1 `#[ignore]`d process-aborting reproducer to
+`tests/parse.rs` (module `hegel_properties`), using the hegel PBT library
+(`hegeltest` 0.28.2 dev-dependency; `cargo add --dev hegeltest` worked
+directly on this edition-2024, rust-version-1.92 crate). The centerpiece is a
+recursive generator over parser-canonical `Edn` values (all 15 variants:
+nil/bool/Int/UInt/Double/Rational/Str/Char/Key/Symbol/Vector/List/Map/Set/
+Tagged) driving a serialize→parse→re-serialize fixpoint property, plus a
+noise-injecting emitter that re-serializes any generated value with random
+whitespace, commas, `;` comments and `#_` discard forms between tokens.
+
+**Six real bugs were found** (five pinned by always-failing hegel tests, one
+process-aborting stack overflow kept as an `#[ignore]`d reproducer). Sibling
+properties exclude the known-bug regions with comments referencing the pins
+and stay green.
+
+Full run: `cargo test --test parse` — 20 passed, 5 failed (the intentional
+pins), 1 ignored. The same numbers hold under `--no-default-features` (the
+`sets`-feature code paths are cfg-gated in the tests). Code passes the
+repo's pre-commit gate (`cargo fmt --check` + clippy pedantic/nursery
+`--deny warnings` on `--all-targets --all-features`).
+
+## 2. Properties added (10)
+
+All in `tests/parse.rs::hegel_properties`. The shared generator
+`edn_values(depth)` produces parser-canonical values: non-negative integers
+as `UInt` (the parser tries `u64` first), negative as `Int`, doubles
+non-integral and finite, strings/chars/symbols avoiding the five pinned bug
+regions (each exclusion commented at the generator with a pointer to its
+pin).
+
+1. `prop_serialize_parse_fixpoint` — for arbitrary nested `Edn`,
+   `Edn::from_str(v.to_string()) == v` and re-serialization is a string
+   fixpoint (set/map ordering is canonical via `BTreeSet`/`BTreeMap`).
+2. `prop_parse_never_panics` — `from_str` returns `Ok`/`Err`, never panics,
+   on arbitrary Unicode text and on EDN-shaped token soup (delimiters,
+   `#_`, radix/hex fragments, `İ`/`٣` multibyte lowercase-shifting chars,
+   unterminated strings...).
+3. `prop_deeply_nested_collections_parse` — balanced `[[[...]]]`,
+   `((...))`, `{:k {:k ...}}`, `#{#{...}}` mixes parse Ok, sweeping drawn
+   depth 1..=300 (cap protects the test process from the pinned stack
+   overflow at ~depth 1000, not a library contract).
+4. `prop_integer_decimal_roundtrip` — decimal integers across the full
+   i64/u64 ranges (MIN/MAX/0/±1 boosted) parse to the exact expected
+   variant/value, including the EDN-spec redundant `+` prefix.
+5. `prop_radix_integer_parse` — `NrDIGITS` (radix 2–36, upper/lowercase
+   digits, negative forms) and `0x` hex literals parse to the value computed
+   by an independent base-conversion oracle in the test.
+6. `prop_string_escape_roundtrip` — strings generated at the escape-set
+   boundary (quotes, backslashes, `\n \r \t`, delimiters, `;`, `#`, full
+   Unicode) round-trip.
+7. `prop_char_roundtrip` — char literals round-trip, including named ones
+   (`\newline \return \tab \space`) and delimiter/lookalike chars
+   (`\]`, `\n` the letter, `\,`...).
+8. `prop_keyword_roundtrip` — keywords incl. namespaced (`:foo/bar`) parse
+   to `Edn::Key` and serialize back to the same text.
+9. `prop_symbol_roundtrip` — symbols incl. namespaced (`foo/bar`) parse to
+   `Edn::Symbol` and serialize back identically.
+10. `prop_whitespace_comment_discard_insensitivity` — a custom emitter
+    re-serializes any generated value inserting random whitespace variants,
+    commas, `; comments` (containing delimiters) and `#_ <elem>` discard
+    forms at legal token boundaries; the parse result must equal the
+    original value.
+
+## 3. Real bugs found (pinned, deliberately failing)
+
+### Bug 1: whole-number doubles serialize without `.0` and reparse as integers
+`known_bug_whole_double_reparses_as_integer` —
+`Edn::Double(3.0).to_string() == "3"`, which reparses as `Edn::UInt(3)`.
+EDN distinguishes `3` from `3.0`; every integral double silently changes
+type through a serialize/parse cycle. Related (same `Display` impl):
+`Double(f64::NAN)`/`Double(INFINITY)` serialize as `NaN`/`inf`, which are
+not valid EDN (spec: `##NaN`/`##Inf`) and reparse as symbols.
+Shrunk counterexample: `Double(0.0)` → `"0"` → `UInt(0)`.
+
+### Bug 2: strings with control characters serialize to unparseable EDN
+`known_bug_control_char_in_string_does_not_reparse` — `Edn::Str` serializes
+via Rust's `{:?}` formatting, which escapes control characters as
+`\u{7f}`-style escapes; the parser only understands `\t \r \n \\ \"` and
+returns `Err("Invalid escape sequence \\u")`. Any string containing a
+control character cannot round-trip. Counterexample:
+`Str("a\u{7f}b")` → `"\"a\\u{7f}b\""` → parse error.
+
+### Bug 3: char literals for non-ASCII whitespace are emitted but rejected
+`known_bug_unicode_whitespace_char_does_not_reparse` — `Edn::Char('\u{a0}')`
+(NBSP; likewise NEL, LINE/PARAGRAPH SEPARATOR, em-spaces...) serializes as
+`\<char>`, but `read_char` collects the literal with
+`take_while(!is_whitespace)`, sees an empty element and errors
+(`"\"\" could not be parsed as a symbol"`).
+
+### Bug 4: hex/radix digit strings that look like float notation misparse as doubles
+`known_bug_radix_digits_with_e_misparse_as_double` — after stripping the
+`0x`/`NNr` prefix, `read_number` checks the remaining digits against float
+notation *first*, so `0x5e5` parses as `Double(500000.0)` instead of
+`UInt(1509)`; `0x0e0` → `Double(0.0)` instead of `UInt(224)`. Affects any
+radix ≥ 15 whose digit string matches `<digits>e<digits>`.
+
+### Bug 5: a single-character symbol at end of input fails to parse
+`known_bug_single_char_symbol_at_eof_fails` — `Edn::from_str("a")` returns
+`Err(ParseEdn("Could not identify symbol index"))`: `read_symbol` (and
+`read_bool_or_nil`) unconditionally demand a lookahead character to compute
+an error-message index. `"a "` parses fine. Found by the properties at the
+default case count (`Symbol("A")`), and the 10x exploratory run found the
+nested form `"#A A"` (tagged element whose value is a 1-char symbol —
+tagged values have no closing delimiter, so the symbol ends at EOF).
+
+### Bug 6 (process-aborting): unbounded parser recursion — stack overflow
+`known_bug_deep_nesting_stack_overflow` (`#[ignore]`d) — the parser recurses
+once per nesting level with no depth limit; `"[".repeat(1000) + "]".repeat(1000)`
+(likewise `{:a {:a ...}}` and `#{#{...}}` nesting) overflows the 2 MiB
+test-thread stack and **aborts the whole process** (SIGABRT), a classic
+untrusted-input DoS for a parser. Depth 800 still parses. Verified by an
+external sweep (1000/2000/5000/10000/50000 all abort for all three
+collection kinds). Kept ignored because a stack overflow cannot be caught
+and would take down the suite; the live property sweeps depth ≤ 300.
+
+Not pinned as a bug, but a roundtrip caveat worth knowing: the parser
+canonicalizes every non-negative integer to `Edn::UInt` (it tries `u64`
+before `i64`), so `Edn::Int(5)` serializes to `"5"` and reparses as
+`UInt(5)`. The generator produces parser-canonical values (negative `Int`
+only), treating this as intended normalization rather than a defect.
+
+## 4. Verification (Run and Reflect)
+
+- **Liveness**: temporarily inverted assertions in three properties
+  (fixpoint equality, the *guarded* negative-radix branch, and noise
+  insensitivity); all three failed with fully shrunk minimal
+  counterexamples (`Nil`, and `v=1, radix=2` inside the `iv > 0` guard —
+  proving the guarded branch fires), then reverted and `.hegel/` cleared.
+- **10x exploratory run**: all `prop_` tests temporarily raised to
+  `test_cases = 1000`, with a per-case atomic counter on the fixpoint
+  property proving >1000 cases actually executed. This run discovered the
+  nested `"#A A"` variant of Bug 5, after which the exclusion helper was
+  generalized (`ends_with_single_char_symbol`) and the 10x run went green.
+  Committed tests are back at the default count.
+- Pins are deterministic: each pinned generator produces a failing input on
+  every case (e.g. every integral double fails Bug 1), so the tests fail on
+  every run, not probabilistically.
+- `.hegel/` added to `.gitignore`.
+
+## 5. Exclusions, limitations, and notes
+
+- **Known-bug exclusions in green tests** (each commented in code with a
+  pointer to its pin): doubles are forced non-integral and finite (Bug 1);
+  string chars are restricted to Debug-verbatim chars plus the five
+  supported escapes (Bug 2); chars exclude non-ASCII whitespace (Bug 3);
+  radix digit strings matching float notation are `assume`d away (Bug 4);
+  documents ending in a single-char symbol are `assume`d away (Bug 5); the
+  nesting property caps depth at 300 (Bug 6 — protects the test process,
+  not a contract).
+- Symbols/keywords are generated from a conservative EDN-name alphabet
+  (letter-first, `[A-Za-z0-9_.*+!?$%&=<>-]`, optional single `/`
+  namespace). The parser accepts far laxer names (anything up to
+  whitespace/delimiter); the lax fringe is exercised by the no-panic soup
+  test rather than roundtrip properties.
+- `Edn::Map` keys are stored as `String` in serialized form; the generator
+  draws keyword-, integer- and quoted-string-shaped keys. Discard (`#_`)
+  noise is injected in vector/list/top-level positions only — in map
+  key/value slots a trailing discard can produce `Edn::Empty` values
+  (arguably a further parser quirk, not investigated to a pin).
+- Pre-existing breakage (unrelated to this work, confirmed on the pristine
+  tree): `cargo test --no-default-features` — a Makefile target — fails to
+  compile `tests/deserialize.rs`, `tests/ser.rs` and `examples/serialize`
+  (`hmap!`/`hset!` and `HashMap` serialization require the `std` feature).
+  My test module cfg-gates its `sets` usage and compiles cleanly in that
+  configuration (`cargo test --no-default-features --test parse` runs with
+  the same 20/5/1 results).
+- The crate is deprecated upstream (maintenance halted; README points to
+  `Grinkers/clojure-reader`), which may bound the value of filing these
+  bugs; the parser bugs (2, 4, 5, 6) likely have analogues worth checking
+  in the successor crate.
